MAJOR THEMES SURFACED
================================================================

1. DAEMON SECURITY HARDENING NEEDED
   The new SRR1 daemon shipped without basic hardening:
   - World-connectable socket (P0)
   - No peer-cred check
   - No oversized-request bounds
   - No per-method authorization
   - Unbounded thread spawn (DoS)
   - Silent panic swallow
   - No structured logging / audit trail
   - TOCTOU at socket setup/teardown
   - Schema deserializer accepts malformed inputs
   These are correctable but constitute a meaningful security debt before
   the daemon should be exposed to multi-agent traffic.

2. SCHEMA REGISTRATION GAPS
   The ee.daemon.* schemas are present as JSON files but were not wired into:
   - schema_drift::all_schemas (CI gate)
   - EffectManifest declarations
   - Failure-mode fixture catalog (7 new degraded codes missing fixtures)
   - docs/degraded_code_taxonomy.md
   Per AGENTS.md: "Adding a feature without updating its gate is a regression."

3. CACHE COHERENCE MISSING
   SpeculativePrefetch + lexical_ram_tier have no shared invalidation token:
   - No (workspace_generation, index_generation) coherence pair
   - Stale prefetch hits after index regen
   - CassPrefetchMetrics has no reset()/clear

4. CLOSURE-LINT REGEX GAPS
   bd-mtbjh (R1) flagged closure-lint regex misses *_NOT_IMPLEMENTED_C... ;
   bd-3fl1p (R3) VERIFIED this still gates broken. The Round-5 closures
   themselves may have implements-surface vs honesty-only taxonomy violations
   (bd-2xpg5, bd-367d7).

5. ADR DRIFT
   The 3 research-defer ADRs (0048/0049/0050) use 3 different Status
   vocabularies, 3 different Reopen Criteria header styles, and 0048 is
   missing Consequences + Rejected Alternatives sections vs 0047 template.

6. SUPPLY-CHAIN AUDIT GAP
   No cargo-deny invocation in CI; advisory-db unverified for new dev-deps
   introduced with the daemon / Sigstore release path. Sigstore cert
   identity regexp trusts release.yml on refs/heads/...

7. BENCHMARK NON-EXISTENCE
   The daemon's reason-for-existence is "cold-start latency for ee context".
   No benchmark exists proving the daemon actually delivers that benefit.

